Bytes removed by shrinking a file no longer come back after the server stops: a shrink that landed while the server was committing could be stored with its new size but not its cut, so after a crash or a clean shutdown the removed bytes could be read again, at once or after the file grew; files an earlier version stored that way are not repaired.
A -read-only mount of a bucket that holds no filesystem exits with an error, where before it created a new, empty filesystem there and served that, and in a bucket whose root pointer had been lost could delete some of the snapshots left behind; only a mount without -read-only creates a filesystem.
BREAKING: strata exits with an error, before it touches the bucket, when -uid or -gid is above 4294967294 or negative other than the default -1, where before a value from 4294967296 up silently wrapped to a different id, so that -uid 4294967296 made root the owner of a new filesystem's root directory, 4294967295 gave it an owner a Linux NFS client rejects, and any other negative value meant the current user. A command line carrying such a value must be corrected, including one that mounts an existing filesystem, whose owner the flags never change, or runs -check.
A new filesystem left at the default -uid or -gid is owned by the uid and gid strata runs as. Before, it was owned by root whenever the current user could not be looked up, as when strata ran under a uid with no passwd entry, and its group was the user's primary group even when strata ran under another group.
BREAKING: strata exits with an error, before it touches the bucket, when -commit-interval is negative, where before a writable mount printed its mount instructions and then crashed, having already created the filesystem if the bucket was empty, a -read-only mount ran with the value unused, and -check ignored the value and probed the endpoint. A -read-only or -check command line carrying a negative -commit-interval must be corrected.
BREAKING: strata exits with an error, before it touches the bucket, when -chunk-size is negative or above 4194303 KiB, where before it silently wrapped the value to a different chunk size, which a new filesystem then kept for good along with the maximum file size that follows from it: -chunk-size 4194308 made 4 KiB chunks and a 4 GiB limit. A command line carrying such a value must be corrected even when it mounts an existing filesystem, which keeps its own chunk size whatever the flag says.
A -cache value too large in either direction to express in bytes no longer wraps: above 8796093022207 MiB the chunk cache is effectively unbounded, as it already was just below that, and below -8796093022208 MiB it gets the 256 MiB default, as every other negative value already did, where before such a value could give the cache any size, often the 256 MiB default or 1 MiB.
BREAKING: A file can be at most 2^20 chunks long, which is 1 TiB at the default 1 MiB chunk size and scales with the -chunk-size a filesystem was created with, and FSINFO advertises that limit instead of an unenforced 2^62: a SETATTR or CREATE asking for a larger size fails with NFS3ERR_FBIG, and a WRITE stops at the limit, failing with NFS3ERR_FBIG if it starts there, where before one request could exhaust the server's memory and a WRITE running past offset 2^64 overwrote the start of the file. A file an earlier version left larger can still be shrunk to the limit or removed, but not written past it, and a Linux client reads it only up to the limit; strata logs a warning at mount when it finds one, so copy such files elsewhere before upgrading, or afterwards with the earlier version, which still mounts the bucket.
Growing a file after shrinking it reads zeros where the removed bytes were: when the shrink cut into data the server had not cached, those bytes could reappear once the file grew again, and a write into the shortened part or a second shrink before the next commit could store them in the bucket for good.
Shrinking files no longer builds up memory between commits: each shrink that ended inside a chunk could cost up to a chunk of memory outside -max-dirty, held until or allocated at the next commit, so enough size changes, even on one empty file, could exhaust the server's memory without any data being written.
Reads no longer return data older than a write that had already been acknowledged, or a mix of data from before and after one write: a read that overlapped a commit of the same file could return the file's previous contents, and a read that overlapped a write to it could return part of that write.
Reads no longer return wrong data after a commit fails partway: a later write to the same file could change a chunk the cache was serving, so other files holding that chunk read the changed bytes until the server restarted, and writing to or truncating one of them could store those bytes in the bucket.
The chunk cache now stays within its -cache size; each chunk it kept after a commit was counted at its length but held the whole chunk-size write buffer, so after many small files it could use many times the memory configured.
Buffered write data is bounded by a new -max-dirty flag (MiB, default 256; 0 or less for no limit): once unflushed writes hold that much memory, further writes wait while the server commits, and fail if that commit fails.
Acknowledged data is no longer silently lost; a write that arrived while the periodic commit or a client COMMIT was flushing the file could discard earlier writes to the same chunk, even ones a COMMIT had confirmed.
A FILE_SYNC write returns instead of hanging; the hung write blocked every later COMMIT, periodic commit and shutdown, so nothing further was committed to the bucket.
A DATA_SYNC write is committed to the bucket before the reply and answered FILE_SYNC, instead of being answered UNSTABLE, which RFC 1813 forbids.
A non-idempotent NFS request retransmitted on the same connection gets the original reply instead of being executed a second time; a duplicate request cache remembers recent replies.
Chunks are verified against the hash that names them when fetched from the bucket; a mismatch fails the operation with NFS3ERR_IO. New -verify-chunks flag, default on.
